#passage(("Even in the Hadith traditions, there are some liars that attributed the most obvious fabrications through really well-known isnad, through well-known sources. Those were those that were not professionals at what they were doing. While the smarter liars, they would attribute reports to sources that were not as famous. They would come up with a chain of narrators that you've never heard of before. And that way, it wouldn't be as suspicious. You would maybe suspect the source instead of suspecting them.", "Everyone attributes a Hadith to Abu Hurairah, whether they're authentic or weak. But let's assume you attribute it to some unknown companion that doesn't really have many Hadith. The likelihood of you getting away with that may be greater. Maybe the spotlight will be on the chain up to him instead of you.", "There is a British theologian called Dennis Nineham, who has a book on the Gospel of Mark, and he claims that Mark was the most common name in Rome.", "So the earliest source that talks about this stuff in detail is a fourth century scholar who holds a position. He doesn't mention the possible existence of the other position, and therefore biblical scholars today assume that the attribution to Mark is correct.", "The first Gospel I've ever read was Mark. I still haven't read Matthew. I still haven't read Luke. I've just read Mark and John, and the thing that stuck out to me is there was no \"I said this to Jesus\" or \"he told me\"; there was none of that, and that was the first thing that stuck in my mind when I was done. It's just really interesting that anyone would try to argue otherwise after that. I'm so used to every single Hadith hearing the word [haddathana] or [akhbarana], he told me, I heard from him. This is just how Hadiths work. I don't know about Matthew and Luke, but Mark doesn't have a single instance of him saying I saw Jesus or Jesus told me or any of that.", "If I was there at the time, I would stick this in the first paragraph and I would say this is the stuff that Peter said to me. I would do that to give this work some weight.", "That's just silly. This would never fly in the Hadiths: we don't know who the source is, but he criticized the narrator, that means he must have been there. It's just absolutely absurd. I understand why they make these arguments, but just because someone makes argument does not mean it's a good argument. The fact that this argument was made gives me a lot of doubt about the authenticity of this work.", "Couldn't one say that the author of Mark may have been influenced by Acts?",), "Farid Responds, “Who wrote the Gospel of Mark?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xybQmzH07MY", ((1508, "25:08–26:01"), (1570, "26:10–26:34"), (1761, "29:21–29:55"), (1955, "32:35–33:04"), (2093, "34:53–36:01"), (2180, "36:20–36:34"), (2306, "38:26–39:11"), (2524, "42:04–42:11"),), id: 4500)
