#passage(("Do Muslims believe the Bible is corrupted? A leading Christian scholar when it comes to defending the Bible, this is the best person that Christianity has to offer. He's called Daniel B. Wallace. He's undoubtedly the most important scholar when it comes to textual criticism and defending the Bible and Christianity. He's the one who people would bring to debate the likes of [Ehrman]. You hear a lot of Muslim quoting [Ehrman] to talk about the corruption of the Bible. So, Daniel [Wallace] will be the person who will argue against [Ehrman] to defend the Bible.", "A clear admission from Daniel B. Wallace. This person is a Christian fanatic. He's zealous for Christianity. He's a student of Bruce [Metzger] who would do the same thing. This guy is telling you all the manuscripts that Christians have are corrupted. There's no perfect manuscripts. All of the manuscripts are corrupted. There's some that are better than others and we have to subjectively look at them and investigate and see what's going on here.", "To give you just an overview, when it comes to the Bible and textual criticism there are two schools: a school that is led by people like Bruce [Metzger], Daniel Wallace, Philip Comfort. These people are people who are going to argue that we can restore the text of the Bible. We can restore the text of that which was written attributed to Mark and some of the disciples. These are the Christian scholars of biblical textual criticism that defend Christianity. [Wallace] is one of the most zealous and he's on that side. Then you have another side: these are people like [Ehrman] who isn't Christian, but you have people like David Parker. You have people like Keith Elliot and [Ian Moir]. These people are Christians but they would argue that you cannot restore the text of the Bible.", "I'm not going to use the skeptical textual criticism scholars like [Ehrman], like you see many Muslims are doing, or David Parker. I'm going to use the conservative Christian scholars, the best of what Christianity has to offer to defend the Bible. Why? Because I'm going to show you that even the most zealous among the Christians and the people who try everything to defend Christianity would still admit to biblical corruption. And it's also because we want to give Christians the benefit of the doubt. Something that they never give us as Muslims. They always look for the worst interpretation for some random unknown person, a person who's not specialized.", "What is textual criticism? Daniel [Wallace] is telling us that the New Testament textual criticism means to try and recover the original text. If we had the original text to begin with, we wouldn't need textual criticism. And now new textual criticism scholarship is not just to try and recover the original text but it's also to study the history of [the scribes], the people who transmitted the Bible, how they changed the text, the religious background, their political and social economic status, religious beliefs, reasons and motivations for changing.", "So essentially Wallace is telling us that, A, we don't have the original text; B, that the manuscripts we have contradict each other, because if they all agreed with each other, according to Daniel Wallace, we wouldn't need textual criticism. And the purpose of textual criticism is to subjectively attempt, because now this will rely on subjective estimation, to recover the original text.", "Now that we defined what textual criticism is, what do we mean when we use the word corruption in the text? Daniel is telling us there are five different types of corruption happening to the text, happening to the manuscripts: addition, omission, alteration, complete replacement and change of the text. And he tells you that the change goes from one word to 12 words. And he tells you that there's two places in the New Testament where this actually happens.", "Do you realize how much of the meaning can change if we change one letter? Let me give you an example. Let's say if we substitute a letter in the word Lord, change the letter L to the letter W, then it becomes what? So the word word becomes Lord and the word Lord becomes word. So that's just one letter, and now imagine applying that change to the text of the Bible itself that we have today. So John 1:1, it says in the beginning was the word, but let's change now the word word to Lord. So in the beginning was the Lord, the Lord was with God, the Lord was God. Complete change in meaning. Now we can apply this to many places in the text.", "Let's talk about changing a word now: look at places in the Bible, in the New Testament, and try and change the word father in the Bible with the word Judas. So now it's one word, it's one change, but you literally are going to make Judas the God of the Bible in some places. The change is so massive if we're talking about just a letter or a word, but you have an admission from Daniel Wallace that you have as many as 12 words being changed and substituted and corrupted in the manuscripts of the Bible that we have today. If this doesn't mean corruption, then what does corruption even mean?", "Now we have tens of thousands of these manuscripts. How many variants/corruptions do you think are in these manuscripts? And the answer is 400 to 500,000.", "There are some Christians who, if they hear these numbers, would be thinking: oh, that doesn't matter, this is really nothing. They would be like: you're talking about all these changes, but they actually don't change the meaning of the text, they don't change the core doctrine. Or they're going to tell you that you have, for example, 2,000 manuscripts: [1,000] manuscripts says the word Jesus and one other thousand manuscripts says the word Lord, and then they count this as 1,000 variants. Therefore, when we talk about 400,000, 500,000, they're not that many, because when you look at the [variants], they're actually very small changes, or we're talking about scribal errors. Maybe someone was typing Jesus, but he put teases or something, a T instead of J, and then they will say that's a variant in the manuscripts.", "I'm going to address all of these things one by one, and I'm going to show you how absurd these claims are. But in reality, this is what a lot of Christians believe today. This is what they're told in the church. This is what they hear from their priests. Just like this absurd idea: we don't have different Bibles, we have different translations. No, you have different Bibles. You don't have different translations, and these different Bibles are based on different manuscripts. And these different translations have different wordings because of the manuscripts. Now we're talking about the Greek manuscripts, which all of the translations that you have in your hand rely on.", "Let's start first with the nonsensical claim that these are actually scribal errors, unintentional mistakes. They were not done with a real purpose. The people who were changing were not maliciously doing this. People who were changing were not changing it to support their beliefs.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The Christian Dilemma: Do Muslims Believe The Bible Is Corrupted? Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3bEZW_kx7Xg", ((19, "0:19–1:09"), (97, "1:37–2:13"), (146, "2:26–3:12"), (198, "3:18–3:54"), (253, "4:13–5:30"), (344, "5:44–6:11"), (393, "6:33–9:27"), (567, "9:27–10:12"), (619, "10:19–11:04"), (664, "11:04–11:29"), (689, "11:29–12:46"), (766, "12:46–13:30"), (820, "13:40–13:58"),), id: 4545)
