#passage(("Daniel Wallace, he's telling us that these scribes, when they found something that contradicted another gospel, because they had these similar gospels, they intentionally edit it. They changed it to harmonize the meaning of the text. So it's intentional changes to support their belief. Their belief is that this book is inspired. So it shouldn't have contradictions. So we're intentionally going to make changes for religious reasons to avoid contradictions in the text.", "If you want to look at actual examples from manuscripts, I'd recommend some books. For example, you have Orthodox Corruption of the Scripture written by [Ehrman]. He brings you a manuscript and he brings you the corruption. You can look at it yourself. Also there's a great book by Dr. Sami [Ameri]. It's called Hunting for the Word of God. If you go to page 110 or 219, he'll bring the Greek manuscripts and the Greek words. He'll also bring you the Christian scholar commentary. Then he will show you how these changes are done to harmonize the text and to avoid contradictions and for religious meanings. These changes that we're talking about that are happening in the manuscripts were not just simple [scribal] errors for no intentions and simple words.", "So what about the second claim? These mistakes are actually counted, [1,000] Jesus and [1,000] Lord, and they tell us actually that's a thousand variant in the manuscripts. This nonsensical idea they spread by many preachers is completely false. So when we talk about these variants in the manuscripts of the Bible today, he tells you literally two and a half [variants] and changes for each word in the New Testament. We were talking about a change in one letter, let alone now the change in one word, let alone the change in more. None of this is true. We're talking about actual variants now.", "So now you have another claim that people will tell you: that these actual changes are in later manuscripts. There's an explanation, and the explanation is we have so many manuscripts that came later on; most of these manuscripts have [variants] and problems, but the earlier manuscripts are actually better. They are the ones we can rely upon, and they are the ones that is going to save us from all of these [variants] and confusion. So we have here Daniel Wallace telling us that the earliest and closest two manuscripts you can find, the best possible matches among manuscripts, you're literally looking at a couple thousand differences. We're talking about the best you can possibly find. And remember, Daniel is the final boss when it comes to defending a New Testament.", "What about the idea that even though we have these changes actually doesn't matter because they don't change the meaning? They'll tell you not only that they don't change the meaning, but they don't change the doctrine. The doctrine of Christianity is preserved. I'm just giving you now the one who's on the forefront, the leader of defending the Bible. He's saying more than 25% of the changes in the manuscripts, we're talking about more than 100,000 changes, they change the meaning of the text significantly.", "When he says that changes are not viable, then he means that these changes are not likely to represent what the original text was. So there are changes and they change the meaning, but they're not likely, if we compare the manuscripts, to represent what the original text was. So literally he's telling you there's over 100,000 explicit corruptions that significantly change the meaning that are not from the original text. I don't understand how someone can come to a Muslim or to anyone and be like, okay, the Bible is not corrupted, or how a Christian can listen to someone like this and still think in his mind, oh, there's maybe a possibility that this is a historically reliable book.", "Someone might come and say: I'll agree with you, there is all of these corruptions, over 100,000 corruptions that significantly change the meaning and not from the original text, but it doesn't matter, because it doesn't represent the original text and probably doesn't influence the doctrine. So Daniel [Wallace] continues. So now he says 4,000 changes that both change the meaning significantly and are likely to represent the original text. So now we are thinking that these are actually representing what the original text was that we're trying to reach. But we still have these 4,000 significant changes. Before he mentioned 500,000 and he changed it to 400,000, so you could be like 1% is actually 4,000, not 5,000.", "Let's be nice with Danny Wallace, who's zealous for Christianity. Let's be nicer and say it's 2,000 changes that are likely to represent the original text, that are important changes that make a difference among these critics that are even zealous for defending Christianity. You understand how much a text can change? I gave an example in the beginning about one letter and one word. Now imagine 2,000 differences. And remember, the New Testament doesn't have the creeds of Christianity. It doesn't tell you that chapter one, there are three in one, the trinity; chapter two, Jesus is God. You don't have that. It's stories and parables. And so if you change a little bit in these stories and parables, just change a few words, just change a word, a few letters, you have a completely different belief. You have a completely different creed. So when someone comes and says, \"Oh, this doesn't affect the creed of Christian,\" that's just a bunch of nonsense. And this is from the admission of their conservative scholars.", "If you want to go back to certain books and look at the examples yourself, I'll give you a few references that you can look at. These books have these changes that are significant changes and likely to represent the text; they will show, literally almost in every page. So for example you have the UBS, the fifth revised edition, which is the [United Bible Societies]: even though this is from conservative Christians, almost in every page they will show you differences. You can get the Nestle [Aland] NA28, for example, serves the same purpose, or you can buy Bruce [Metzger], his book A Textual Commentary, or Philip Comfort, he also has a textual commentary on the manuscript and text of the New Testament. These people, which are the conservative scholars, are going to show you these significant changes that are changing the meaning and are viable as well.", "There are books written just about one gospel because of how many changes there are. So you have for example The Text of the New Testament in Greek: The Gospel According to St. Luke, by the American and British international committee. They show you these changes that influence and affect the meaning, and they're just focusing on one gospel because there's so many changes.", "Even though we're saying these are corruptions and the scholars admit they're corruptions, they exist in the Bible today. And even if some of these Bibles remove some of these verses or put a footnote or whatever, other Bibles have them. And people read them. And most people don't look at the footnotes anyways. So they're reading and thinking this is the word of God. Even they themselves tell you, those conservative scholars, these are corruptions; they still leave these verses in their book. It's crazy.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The Christian Dilemma: Do Muslims Believe The Bible Is Corrupted? Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3bEZW_kx7Xg", ((986, "16:26–16:59"), (1044, "17:24–18:02"), (1082, "18:02–22:04"), (1330, "22:10–24:02"), (1452, "24:12–25:28"), (1541, "25:41–26:17"), (1583, "26:23–28:00"), (1684, "28:04–29:03"), (1754, "29:14–30:00"), (1811, "30:11–30:30"), (1848, "30:48–31:13"),), id: 4546)
