#passage(("Daniel B. Wallace: he's the leading scholar for textual criticism for defense of the Bible for conservative Christian. So I'm bringing you the scholars that are on your side. I'm not going to bring you the skeptical scholars. There's two groups of scholars. There is skeptical scholars. These are people like Bart Ehrman. I don't use Bart Ehrman because Bart Ehrman is someone who used to be Christian, became agnostic. I'm not going to use a skeptical scholar. David C. Parker is another example. Daniel Wallace argues, debates against Bart Ehrman to prove the Bible. So he's your side. He is a conservative scholar.", "So you have the skeptical and the conservatives. I'm giving you now the number one conservative scholar, defender of the Bible, Daniel B. Wallace. You can also Google Bruce Metzger. By the way, he was the mentor of Daniel B. Wallace. Philip Comfort: another name. So, these are the three leading scholars defending the Bible and textual criticism Christianity. These are the conservative ones. These are the ones that argue that we can perhaps maybe restore the original text.", "Now, let's talk about what Daniel B. Wallace says. Daniel B. Wallace says, \"Every single manuscript we have of the Bible is corrupted.\" And by the way, there is hundreds of thousands of manuscripts. He says, \"Every single manuscript we have of the Bible is corrupted. There is not any perfect manuscript. We have to look at these manuscripts as corrupt documents and try to come up with the understanding of what the original text perhaps may have been.\" Daniel B. Wallace tells you, \"Every single manuscript contradict each other.\" They are not only all corrupted, but they're all contradicting each other. And Daniel Wallace tells you that it's not the new manuscripts. He says, \"If you go the earliest manuscripts we have, all of them contradict each other.\"", "Daniel B. Wallace talks about corruption of the Bible. He talks about addition and omission. He says, \"This can range from one word to 12 verses.\" You realize how much one word can change in the meaning, let alone 12 verses. And then he says, \"We have two instances already in the New Testament where we have corruption of 12 verses.\" Of course one is in Mark, the ending of Mark, and the other is in John, at the end of chapter 7 and beginning of chapter 8. These are 12 verses, clear instance of 12 verses are completely corruption and are added into scripture.", "And then he says: what is then a corruption? What is a variant? He says some people think that a variant is like the word Jesus and the word Lord. And if we have 1,000 the word Jesus and 1,000 the Lord words, then we have 1,000 variants. Or it's like the word Jesus and Osis, for example O instead of J, or something like that. Then he says no. If we have 1,000 manuscripts that says Jesus and 1,000 manuscripts that says Lord, we consider this as one variant, only one. So he says it's not like people think it is. He says 400 to 500,000. So he says for each word in the New Testament we have two times and a half number of corruptions.", "Then he categorizes corruptions, because not all corruptions are the same. He says, we categorize them into corruptions that change the meaning and corruptions that don't change the meanings, and we categorize them into changes that are viable. Essentially, maybe they represent the text of what Mark says, cuz everything goes back to Mark. So, he says that if they're viable, then they're likely to represent what Mark said. Whoever wrote Mark, which we don't know, whatever that text that was written 70 years after Jesus or so, whatever was written there, it is likely that this is what was written. That's what viable means.", "So, now, let's talk about the corruptions and the changes that are non-viable but meaningful. He says, significantly change the meaning. Not just a small change; significant change in meaning. Of the 400 to 500,000, we have meaningful corruptions of the Bible that are non-viable: over 25%. Essentially, more than 125,000 corruptions in the Bible that significantly change the meaning. It's like, for instance, instead of saying I worship God, I put Judas: I worship Judas. Just one word, but he says the corruptions are not one word. He says up to 12 verses. There is no way you can know what the beliefs are, what Jesus said, what Jesus didn't say, cuz what you have in your hand is a book filled with corruption.", "Now, even the changes that are meaningful but viable, meaning they're likely, in his belief, they're likely to present the original text. By the way, this is a conservative scholar. So, Bart Ehrman would completely disagree. He says there's no way you can even restore anything of the [original]. That would be his position. But I'm bringing you someone who believes that there is a chance you can do it. He believes that there is at least 5,000 variants. But this is, by the way, he's just dumbing down the number as much as possible. But this is 5,000, from one word to 12 verses, changes that are viable. So, if we have 5,000 changes that are viable, significantly change the meaning, there's no way we could know what the original is saying. It's impossible.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Leaves Confident Christian Speechless After Challenging His Bible! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A3kzMpLxIDI", ((768, "12:48–14:36"), (878, "14:38–15:35"), (940, "15:40–16:27"), (995, "16:35–17:33"), (1055, "17:35–18:55"), (1146, "19:06–19:58"), (1200, "20:00–21:07"), (1267, "21:07–21:48"),), id: 4549)
