#passage(("Let's talk now about Islam and what Islam says about preservation. The Quran talks about two scriptures. These two scriptures are given to two prophets of God. Quran talks about the Tawra and gospel, if you want to translate the word [Injil]. Quran doesn't mention the Christian Bible. But could these two scriptures be the Bible? Because apparently some Christians think that they are the Bible. So what are these two scriptures that the Quran talks about?", "Let's make this point first, that Christian and Muslim scholars both believe and agree that Moses spoke a form of Hebrew that we don't have today. If you speak Arabic, you'd know how much a language can change, because we have the original Arabic in dictionaries. Look at the Arabic of today, you'd know how much it can change. And we're talking about thousand years. Now talk about Moses, thousands of years separation. And that Jesus spoke Aramaic, this is what most historians, Muslim and Christian scholars agree to. I think this is safe to say, and even Christians wouldn't disagree with this point.", "So first, what is the [Injil]? The Quran describes the injil as the following: number one, a revelation that was sent down by God. Number two, it talks about it being given directly to Jesus. Allah specifically says that the gospel or the injil that we're talking about was revealed from God and it was given directly to Jesus. Number three, it was in the language of Jesus. We just said the language of Jesus was Aramaic. Allah says in the Quran that when he sends a messenger initially to the people, it's going to be in the tongue and the language of the people, so that he can first preach to his people; then the followers of that messenger can go and preach the message in whichever language needed. So we know for a fact that the gospel that was revealed had to have been revealed in Aramaic, so he can teach it to his people in the language of his people. It definitely wasn't Greek.", "Number four, the gospel can be oral or a written tradition. When you look at the Quran, it talks about it being revelation. There's nothing explicit in the Quran that says that it was a written revelation. And the reason I inclined to the position that it was an oral tradition is because we don't have anything from Christian sources or historical sources that tells us that Jesus commanded people to write. There's nothing in the Bible, nothing outside the Bible, where you'd have the statement that Jesus told people: come here and sit and write down the gospel I'm teaching you. So it seems that he was just teaching it, and there are people later on who may have recorded, and whatever they recorded have been corrupt. So this is what the [Injil] is according to the Quran: revelation sent by God, given directly to Jesus, in the language of Jesus, and mainly as an oral tradition.", "Now what about the Torah? The Quran says also that the Tawra is a revelation sent by God. The difference is, here it says that God wrote the Tawra himself and he gave it to Moses. So the Torah is written, unlike the gospel, and it was given directly to Moses. Also the same exists in the hadith, the discussion between Adam and Moses. Adam says to Moses: you are Moses, Allah chose you for his speech and wrote the Tawra for you with his hand. Number three, it was in ancient Hebrew. As we just said, God will send a messenger in the tongue of his people.", "So this is Islamic definition of what Islam refers to as the Tawra and what Islam refers to as the injil or the gospel. From this Islamic definition, we can deduct and conclude that nobody can come and claim that the [Tawrah and Injil] that Islam talks about are the present day Bible or the Bible that Christians believe in. The reasons are already clear. The Bible is based on manuscripts written in Greek and modern Hebrew. So you cannot say that it's the Aramaic [Injil] or the ancient Hebrew Tawra. And you can't say that at all, because the ancient Hebrew Torah, like we said, was written by God on tablets. So if you don't have the tablets, you don't have the Torah. You have whatever that may have been copied.", "Number two, the Bible is written by anonymous authors and doesn't trace back to Jesus or Moses. The New Testament would trace back to the anonymous author of what they refer to as the Gospel of Mark. And the oldest you have of the old testament, not all of it but just majority, the sea scrolls, which traces back to the sect, don't have anything that traces back to Jesus and Moses. So nobody can come and say, I have the [Tawrah] and [Injil] that the Quran is talking about. The bible contains the statements of many people, contains the statements of disciples, Paul, random people, all of these people. It's not recorded statements of just Jesus and recorded statements of just Moses. So knowing this is just a common sense fact, that nobody can say that the Bible is the Tawra and the Gospel the Quran are referring to.", "And to even give you a cherry on the cake, I'm going to mention some Christian scholars who even admit to this idea. For example, you have the Catholic priest [Sidney] Griffith; in his book The Bible in Arabic, says the gospel the Quran confirms is not the gospel that the Christians today recognize. It's not the same. And it says the Quran is following its own model of what the Quran refers to as the gospel, the scripture of God given to Jesus. And he also says that the Quran intends to criticize and correct what modern Christians believe and have in their own hand, because they don't have what we refer to as the gospel. Also, Catholic priest Lewis, he says: we assert that the Tawra at the time of Muhammad, peace be upon him, and the old Muslim scribes are not the [Pentateuch]. The Tawra that we have is not what the Christians have today in the Old Testament. To what has become of the injil in which the Quran speaks, as we've said before, it cannot be one of the canonical gospels.", "So these are admissions from Christian scholars, that they understand it's a common sense. That's why when you see Christians arguing this point, it's just a straw man; it's not based on research. A basic common understanding and basic analysis of what the Quran says about what the gospel is and what the Torah is, and what the Bible is, clearly tell you that it's impossible for you to claim that they are the same thing.", "So now an important question arises, and that question is: where is the original Torah and gospel today? We hear that question a lot. The answer is quite simple. The original Torah and gospel have been lost through continual corruptions and alterations that happened after these prophets were no longer there, Moses and Jesus. Christians cannot even fully recover and restore a writing that was 70 years after Jesus, that were written by an anonymous author. You can't even recover the writing of the Gospel of Mark, whoever wrote it. You don't know who wrote it down. You don't have it; it's lost according to you, and you're trying to recover whatever you can of it, and you have these hundreds of years in gaps, let alone having something from Jesus, or let alone Moses in ancient Hebrew thousands of years ago.", "However, there's an important point here. Even though we believe that the original Torah and the gospel are lost, which, like we demonstrate, Christian, Jewish scholars also believe, parts of the truth or parts of revelations also survived, some remnants of truth and revelation; they survive even up to the life of the [prophet], to the seventh century. So when we say that they're lost, we're talking about the totality and having an original preserved scripture. We're not talking about remnants of truth remaining with the people of the book, because even if they corrupt the scripture, they would corrupt things that they have problematic issues with. They will try to introduce their own beliefs into some places, but they don't have a problem with every single thing the book says. So there is no reason for 100% corruption to occur, and it would be absurd for anyone to claim that. So there are still some truth that was left, the remnants of truth, up until the seventh century, even during the life of the prophet Muhammad.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The Christian Dilemma: Do Muslims Believe The Bible Is Corrupted? Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3bEZW_kx7Xg", ((2962, "49:22–49:49"), (2989, "49:49–50:20"), (3020, "50:20–51:10"), (3077, "51:17–52:09"), (3136, "52:16–52:45"), (3165, "52:45–53:33"), (3218, "53:38–54:23"), (3263, "54:23–55:18"), (3318, "55:18–55:42"), (3342, "55:42–56:50"), (3410, "56:50–57:47"),), id: 4656)
