#passage(("The word Bible and the word Gospel aren't the same thing. Injil is not an Arabised word from Bible, because Bible is Biblos, it's different. Gospel is a different word, and Bible is a completely different word. Bible is a collection of books. That's why even the Arabs would refer to it as الكتاب المقدس (the Holy Book), or whatever term they would use. It's to do with this collection of these different books by these different authors. So that word Bible, its translation, if you want to say, it would be الكتاب المقدس; it wouldn't be the إنجيل. So now this is where the confusion is happening. We don't believe that these descriptions in the Quran about the Torah and Injil are about the Bible. So if you're reading it and thinking this is about the Bible, then there's a lot of issues that are going to come up because of that.", "It's so simple. The Gospel, or Injil, is used in the Bible four times, for Mark, Matthew, Luke, and John. So, Gospel(s). So now you have أناجيل, plural of Injil. They say الأناجيل الأربعة (the 4 Gospels). So you have four Gospels there within what we call the Bible. That's why we already can tell the difference: it's completely two different things. And then you have the Old Testament, which then you would have all of these different categorisations: is it the Torah? Is it just the first five books? Or is it the entire Old Testament? All of these stories. In the end, what the Quran refers to as the Torah is the Tablets that God Himself wrote for Moses (alAaraf7:145). Now even the Bible records it: written with the finger of God (Exodus31:18), that God wrote Himself and gave it to Moses. So whatever these Tablets and Commandments that were written down and directly given to Moses himself, from God, is what we call the Torah, according to the Quranic Tradition.", "Then, the Injil. Allah in the Quran says (alMaida5:46) وَءَاتَیۡنَـٰهُ ٱلۡإِنجِیلَ: 'Isa ibn Maryam, Jesus, we gave him the Injil. So Injil, is one Injil or 4 Anajil? One! So God gave Jesus the Injil, one Injil. Was Mark, Matthew, Luke, and John given to Jesus? Did Jesus even hear about Mark, Matthew, Luke, and John? All of these Anajil, or what you refer to as Anajil, all of them were written years after Jesus. Jesus was not even on earth to see them.", "This is a new thing now they're trying to introduce. What these new people are trying to do is: oh, Muslims say the Injil, they believe in this! No! That's not what we believe in. Go anywhere in the Arab land, in the Arab world, with the Arab Christians: on their Book it would be written الكتاب المقدس. That's what's written on their Book. Now, if we say actually that Book that you have is الإنجيل, this new update that we got from this church, does alinjil include التوراة (atTawrah: The Torah)? How does alInjil include atTawrah? That's why that's not the norm, because what is called الكتاب المقدس, the Bible, includes the Old and the New Testament, what you would call العهد القديم والعهد الجديد. So, in reality, what you call الكتاب المقدس is what's referred to as the Bible. The word Bible and the word Evangelion or Gospel, they are all different. They're not the same.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Arab Christian Shares His Experience With Islam And Christianity! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kEUpYAh5b3E", ((1201, "20:01–21:14"), (1277, "21:17–23:12"), (1404, "23:24–24:03"), (1502, "25:02–26:23"),), id: 4658)
