#passage(("In this chapter [Bukhari] mentions a few words from the Quran, then provides definitions. Now, just by looking at this information, you'd think that only one sentence here is attributed to [Ibn] Abbas and that the rest are definitions. However, upon returning to other works, we come to realize that these statements are not by [Bukhari]; they're actually attributions to [Ibn] Abbas, with the exception of the middle part here in green, which we cannot find in any source. Anyone who just reads it can come to either conclusion, and many people just read this and simply assume that this [is Ibn Abbas] talking.", "Isn't the attribution to Abbas a stronger argument against Islam? No, it's not, because it's a weak attribution. Do you know where to find an authentic chain? [Bukhari] provides two quotes from Abbas: one has an actual chain and one has no chain. The one with a chain falls under the conditions of [Bukhari], an authentic hadith; the one without a chain does not fall under [Bukhari's] conditions. Therefore we accept the one that falls under his conditions, not the one with no source.",), "Farid Responds, “Three Christian Apologists FAIL to Prove Muslim Scholars Affirmed Bible Preservation”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YwpEiPlFL4g", ((593, "9:53–10:52"), (776, "12:56–13:36"),), id: 4690)
