#passage(("The first argument they come up with is the idea of confirmation: the Quran confirms the previous scriptures. What does that mean? According to them, the Quran confirming the previous scriptures means the Quran says that these scriptures are both preserved and also authoritative, and [they cite] many verses in the Quran to try and claim that this is the case. Let's have a look, for example, at the Quran 3:3. Allah says in the Quran: confirming the Tawra and the Gospel, confirming that which came before.", "But the simple problem with this, I would say, lousy argument that those Christians are bringing about the confirmation is that they don't understand what confirmation means. And if you want to argue what the Quran means, of course you want to argue from what Muslims believe. So let's have a look at what scholars meant. Muslim [scholars] always agree and refer to this idea of confirmation, that it's referring to the truth. It's confirming the truth which is in the original Tawra and gospel regarding the prophecies of prophet Muhammad being sent and the Quran being revealed. And, like I said, remnants of truth was still present, and remnants, definitely, description of the [Prophet] was there. So the Quran is confirming this truth, which traces back to the original Torah and the gospel. So it has nothing to do with preservation in the way that the Christians are talking about.", "So, for example, [Ibn Kathir] says: confirming that which came before, meaning the scriptures that was revealed before, from heaven, from God, to the prophets. It confirms the scriptures because it corroborates what they foretold and prophesies about God's promise that prophet Muhammad will be sent and the Quran will be revealed.", "[Al-Tabari], when he talked about this verse, he also said: the lord revealed to you the book, truth regarding that which the people of the Torah and the gospel [differed] upon. He talks about disagreements between the people of the Torah and the gospel. And then he says the Quran is confirming what came before from God's book that he revealed upon his prophets and messengers, the original, because all of that source is one. So there is no contradiction. So he's saying that confirming here is talking about that which was revealed upon the prophets and messengers, not something which was written later on, not something which is written by anonymous authors, because the source of all of this revelation is one, so it agrees with each other.", "And that's why these verses [are] evidence against the Christians, because the next verse, Quran 3:4, what does it say? It explicitly says that this gospel as a guidance for people in the past, so it's not as guidance for people today. And then Allah says, then he revealed the [Furqan], which is one of the names of the Quran, means the differentiator of truth and falsehood. So Allah is saying this: the original ones, they were guidance for people before; now Allah revealed the [Furqan], the differentiator of truth and falsehood, so it can clarify, like we showed, all of these examples in the Quran that corrects the false beliefs of Christians today.", "Another example about the idea of confirming that which came before is, for example, Quran 5:48. So [Ibn Kathir], what does he say? Kathir says: and we have revealed to you the book in truth, meaning with undeniable truth, it is from God, confirming that which came before, meaning the earlier scriptures that included the mention of the prophet Muhammad, his praise, and that he will receive revelation from God, which further increases its truthfulness in the eyes of those who have insight.", "Let me give you one clear example you probably didn't hear before. Allah in the Quran says that he sent down the gospel confirming the Torah. So Allah mentions that when he gave Jesus the gospel, the gospel confirms the Torah. Just like Allah says the Quran confirms that which came before, Allah says when he gave Jesus the gospel it also confirms that which came before. So if the idea of confirmation meant that you accept everything and follow everything in it, and it's fully authoritative, then here Jesus would be saying that you have to follow the Torah and everything in it, and everything in it is authoritative, because the gospel confirms the Torah. But we know the same verse tells us: confirming that which came before me from the Tawra, and to make lawful for you somewhat which has been forbidden for you. So we have this idea here of abrogation, which is following some parts of the Torah and not following others. Even though Allah tells us in the Quran that Jesus confirms the Torah, he still follows parts and leaves parts.", "So this idea of confirming in the Quran, like the example of this verse, chapter 3 of the Quran, verse 50, where in the case of Jesus confirming the Torah using the gospel, doesn't mean following every little thing in the Tawra, but it can mean general confirmation of the Torah, and it can mean following some parts of it and leaving some parts of it. And that's why, when you look at what [al-Tabari] says about this verse, says: confirming that which was before him from the Torah, because Jesus believed in the Tawra and acknowledged it to be from God. Likewise we, for example, believe in the gospel of Jesus and believe it's from God, and that Jesus was a messenger of God. Then he says: likewise all the prophets believed in that which came before them from the books of God and his messengers, even if some of their laws and rulings have [differed], due to God's differentiation between them in that regard.", "So here explicitly tells you confirmation, but in the same time there's different rulings. Confirmation does not contradict the idea of rules and rulings being different from each other. When you say you confirm a previous scripture, doesn't mean you're saying that that full scripture is true, or is fully need to be followed, or whatever was preserved in its original form is authoritative today. That is not what we understand with the term confirmation in the Quran.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The Christian Dilemma: Do Muslims Believe The Bible Is Corrupted? Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3bEZW_kx7Xg", ((4649, "1:17:29–1:18:05"), (4692, "1:18:12–1:19:30"), (4770, "1:19:30–1:19:48"), (4803, "1:20:03–1:20:46"), (4846, "1:20:46–1:21:26"), (4886, "1:21:26–1:21:52"), (4927, "1:22:07–1:23:03"), (4983, "1:23:03–1:23:55"), (5042, "1:24:02–1:24:27"),), id: 4712)
