#passage(("You're misrepresenting our position, because your argument was based on the Islamic perspective. You argued from the Quran. You said the Quran says about the Bible. So you're arguing from our position now. So you cannot argue from our position and then reject parts of what our position says, that same position that we hold. You jumped into our position when it suited you, like claiming that the Quran affirms the Bible or whatever. If you don't take the Quran as an authority, it doesn't matter. You're arguing with me that the Quran says something, then I should accept it. That's called arguing from my worldview. So if you argue from my world view, then you have to continue your argument from my worldview. You cannot then jump outside my world view. That same Quran says that these scriptures have been corrupted. That same Quran says that the Quran is a criterion over these previous scriptures. This is all the Quran.", "It doesn't negate the fact that it's rational and consistent. So that is now why the Christians are the ones who have [dilemmas] in their beliefs, like the dilemma of trying to justify who's this prophet that came to the Arabic people, described in their own book, that they cannot answer.", "Why we accept the Quran as an authority is separate. We don't need the Bible to accept the Quran as an authority. We already prove that the Quran on its own merit to be from God. No Muslim comes and says, \"I believe in the Quran because of the Bible.\" We're saying separately the Quran is proved to be from God. Muslims, they have their own evidences, separate from the Bible, has nothing to do with the Bible. Then, if the Quran is proved to be from God, then we accept the statements it makes.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Attempts To Use A Christian Argument Against Muslim But Fails Miserably! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nv3_9zjCj2Y", ((1326, "22:06–23:19"), (1406, "23:26–23:41"), (1435, "23:55–24:30"),), id: 4719)
