#passage(("The problem that we have today is we have a lot of Christians that lie about Islam. They go online, they misrepresent the verses of the Quran. The verse of the Quran says the following: \"If you are in doubt.\" So now let's say this is referring to Prophet Muhammad, even though his name is not mentioned in the verse, by the way, but let's say it's referring to Prophet Muhammad. It says \"if you are in doubt\". If I say if, which is a conditional if, in the English language, in English grammar it's for conditional things. If I say if something, does that mean that that thing happened or necessarily can happen, or can that thing be still impossible even though I use the word if? Can I use the word if for something that is impossible?", "I'll give you an example now. If I say to you: if I could fly, I would go to the moon. Is it possible for me to fly? So I can use the word if, and I can use it conditionally for something that is impossible. I can say if I was the president of India, I would build a new home, or some random thing. If I was the president, it doesn't mean I'm going to be the president of India ever, because I have to be born in India, I have to be an Indian, for example, or president of America or whatever. So it's an impossible thing, because the laws are against what I'm saying. So I can use if for something impossible. So if the verse is saying \"if you are in doubt\", that does not even mean that is possible for the prophet to doubt. So I can make the claim easily that if here does not indicate that the prophet would even doubt.", "The second thing I'm going to say: we have already a narration from prophet Muhammad. We have narrations in the books of [tafsir], multiple narrations, where the prophet said, \"I don't doubt and I don't ask,\" after this verse was revealed. The prophet doesn't doubt and doesn't ask. That's why this if, which is conditional, is not talking about a situation that happens or will happen. It's just: if this happens.", "Number two, the verse doesn't say \"if you are in doubt, go read the scriptures\". The verse says \"if you are in doubt, ask those who used to read the scripture before you\". Now this is very important, because the language of the Quran is miraculous. Every detail is important. When the Quran says \"used to read the scriptures before you\", why is it saying before you? Why is it saying they used to read the scripture? Why are they not reading it anymore? You could read parts of transmitted scripture, that some of it is corrupt and some of it is there. You could still read. But what the verse is saying is referring to believers from the people of the book. They used to read the scriptures before Prophet Muhammad, because the prophet wasn't there yet. So they would read whatever was accessible to them. When they accepted Islam, they're no longer reading the scripture, because the prophet came. So this verse is also proof that we don't need the previous scriptures, because the verse is clearly saying they used to read the scriptures; they're no longer reading the scripture.", "So here those people are now Muslims, and that is found in all of the books of [exegesis] we have; the books of [exegesis], explanation, all mentions their names. You have people who used to be Jewish. You have people like Salman [al-Farisi]; he used to be Christian. We have many people, and they're mentioned by name in our books, that this verse is referring to them. This is not something I made up on the spot. These Christians are misrepresenting us because they know in our books it says this, but they ignore it. So this is referring to people who became Muslim.", "And I'll tell you why: because Allah is not going to tell the prophet go and ask disbelievers, that Allah in the same book says that they changed their scripture. How can Allah say they lie about Allah, they change their scripture, and then Allah says, \"Oh prophet, go ask those liars who change the scriptures\"? Allah is not going to do that, of course. But Allah is saying, \"Go ask the believers, if you want confirmation about what? About the stories of the previous prophets that we told you, and about the fact that you're mentioned in the previous scriptures.\"", "The evidence for that is context. If you go back to the last few verses, talking about the stories of the prophets, and if you open any book of [exegesis], it will tell you: to ask about his mention, his description in the previous books, and to ask about the knowledge about the prophets, because the Quran confirms that there was prophets sent before prophet Muhammad, like Moses, like Jesus, like Abraham, like all of these prophets. So those people who accepted Islam and used to be Jewish and Christian, if the prophets goes and asked them, they will confirm: we believed in you because you're mentioned in our scripture; we believe it is true that these stories of these prophets were mentioned in our books. They will confirm that. So this verses here is not general, go ask about everything. No, it's specifically about this point.", "So the Quran came on 23 years. In the beginning of the message of the prophet Muhammad many people accepted Islam. This verse was revealed later on, after those people were already Muslims. The verses of the Quran came on 23 years, different chapters, different verses. It was circumstantial revelation. It was periodic revelation, progressive, that is talking about certain events that happened in the life of the prophet. So it took 23 years and it finished in the end of the life of prophet Muhammad. That's why we have Mecca verses that were revealed in Mecca in the first 13 years, and we have Medina verses which was revealed in the last 10 years.", "So, Abu Jafar, the person who authored this book, by the way died over a [thousand] years ago, or [1,200] years ago. This guy died before any of these Christian apologists even thought about coming in existence, or even their ancestors. He says that Allah speaks that: oh prophet Muhammad, if you are ever in doubt that the children of Israel disagreed about your prophethood before we sent you, because they find you written in their books and they know you with the description in their books, [like Abdullah ibn Salam] and the people like him from the people of truthfulness and belief, not the liars and disbelievers among them. You see how clear it is: he's explicitly telling you this is revealed about the believers like [Abdullah ibn Salam], not the disbelievers.", "Now here he will mention [chains] of narration, and they will say: the people who [explain] the Quran, it is [Abdullah ibn Salam]; he was from the people of the book and he believed in the prophet Muhammad. Here is a narration, the other narration I was telling you: he narrates \"if you are in doubt\", he [says] the [prophet] did not doubt and did not ask. Here's a narration, here's another narration; they're all saying the same thing. From [Qatada], here's the same narration. So what I was telling you is literally mentioned there explicitly, in front of everybody, crystal clear. I'm not a genius that came up with this interpretation. The thing is, the people who are telling you these things are genuinely disingenuous people. They know that this is not the explanation of the verses. They know that the verses say opposite to what these liars are saying. But they still lie about our books. They still lie about what the Quran and Sunnah say.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Inquisitive Christian Challenges The Qur'anic Stance On The Gospel! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eBNkZ5-8HIc", ((918, "15:18–16:20"), (991, "16:31–17:42"), (1064, "17:44–18:08"), (1108, "18:28–20:02"), (1202, "20:02–20:42"), (1242, "20:42–21:17"), (1291, "21:31–22:13"), (1363, "22:43–23:20"), (1429, "23:49–24:56"), (1496, "24:56–26:05"),), id: 4746)
