#passage(("Honestly, I don't even know why some of these are seen as prophecies by Avery. But according to Avery, Jesus was pierced for our sins, he carried our sorrows, and God will place our sins unto him, and through his wounds we will be healed. There are a few issues with this.", "First of all, this idea of our sins being placed on Jesus: is this something that has come to pass? Is this something verifiable? Is it something that you can see? Is it tangible? All you're relying on are the claims of some people that the sins of Christians, or the sins of man, have been shifted onto Jesus, and those are just claims. I don't even know who claimed these: early Christian Church fathers, was it Paul, was it Jesus himself? It doesn't really matter; it's not verifiable, and since it's not verifiable you cannot make the claim that this is something that has actually happened. So ultimately we're stuck with the idea that there are verses in Isaiah speaking about someone bearing someone else's sins. That's pretty much it. To conclude that this is Jesus bearing our sins, it's not something that you can prove. Anyone can make the claim that someone is going to Bear someone else's sins. It's just a claim.", "You also said that he will carry [our] Sorrows. Is that something that is verifiable? Are you claiming that sorrow doesn't exist among Christians, or is it referring to the sorrow of the disciples? I'm pretty sure there was sorrow in their lives as well. Again, maybe it's something about the day of judgment, the afterlife: there will no longer be sorrow among Christians. Again, something that you cannot verify; it's not something that has happened yet, it's not something that's tangible.", "And finally, Jesus being pierced for our transgressions. The problem with that is, yeah, he was pierced in the Gospel of John, but that was just to examine whether he was dead or not. It's not that he was pierced for transgressions of man. But let's assume that this is actually referring to what's supposed to happen to the Messiah, even though the Messiah is not mentioned in the verses they're being quoted, but I'll give you that for now. So what are the signs? That he's pierced, that's pretty much it. Again, that could be said about anyone that was pierced, anyone that was stabbed; it's quite the general thing. But here, of course, it's saying pierced for our sins, which, yes, okay, is specific, but it's something that's not tangible, it's something that you cannot examine for yourself. You cannot claim for certain that someone being pierced was pierced to remove the sins of humanity.", "I know what you're thinking: that Jesus was actually being pierced to the Cross. Well, there's a little problem with that: there's no actual accounts of Jesus literally being pierced to the cross, and the Romans themselves would often tie those that they are crucifying. The Gospel of John also claims that none of the bones of Jesus were broken, and I'm not a doctor, but I think that if you had Nails driven through your hands and feet you'd have some bones broken. Regardless, there's no proof about Jesus being nailed to the cross, so I wouldn't be accepting Isaiah 53 as evidence of him being crucified.", "I would strongly recommend checking out the verses for yourself in order to get a feel for whether this is about Jesus or not. But check out the very next verses: \"he was oppressed and Afflicted, yet he did not open his mouth; he was led like a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before a [shearer] is silent, so he did not open his mouth.\" And of course, as we all know, Jesus was not silent as he was heading onto the cross, and while he was on the cross he was shouting \"my God, why have you forsaken me\". So this wouldn't apply to Jesus at all.",), "Farid Responds, “Godlogic's Top Proofs for Christianity Examined”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=omgohXugqN4", ((201, "3:21–3:47"), (227, "3:47–4:55"), (295, "4:55–5:33"), (333, "5:33–6:35"), (397, "6:37–7:20"), (446, "7:26–8:03"),), id: 4773)
