#passage(("Isaiah 53 is not going to work. You were just saying, you have to let the scripture interpret itself. This is what I'd love to do. If there's a Prophecy about Jesus, the WHOLE Prophecy has to fit him. You cannot take a part of the verses and say \"that they do\", and some verses you say \"they don't do.\"", "53, which Christians would claim it's a \"Suffering Servant\" (this is the term they use for it), the Jewish people will tell you it is referring to children of Israel, because they did suffer and they were bruised for iniquities and sin and things that they've done. Because the term \"the servant\", they will tell you, it's used: look at Isaiah 50, 51, 54. They would say to you: look the chapters before and after. This is what the Rabbis would tell you; this is their explanation I'm giving you right now. They'll say to you this is referring to \"Israel\". It's not referring to one individual, or one being, or one entity.", "The term there used for \"seed\" is \"zera'\", which means \"Offspring\", \"Children\", literal children. So it says two things: that God will prolong the days of that whoever is being prophesied for here, so he will live a long life. This is what \"prolonging days\" means. And number two, it says that they will have children: he will see, they will see the children. That entity that is being prophesied is going to see its children. Now when you look at Jesus, he is the exact opposite of these two things. First, dying at the age of 33, according to what Christians believe, so still in his youth; he's a very young man. And he never had married or seen children in his life.", "So why then the Jewish people believe it's about the children of Israel? Because it's the same God is promising them that: you're going to continue, you're not going to get extinct. Because they were small groups, they were always scattered, they've always been attacked. So God is telling them that: I will prolong your days, you're not going to go extinct, and I will increase your numbers. You'll see your children, you'll see your \"zera'\", you'll see your seed. So this is a promise that God has given them. Even though you had all of these afflictions, even though you were bruised, God is promising them as the children of Israel that: no, you will have a long life, you'll have everlasting days, you have more days to live, and I will increase you in numbers. Which is why I would say this chapter cannot fit Jesus.", "So you're going for a metaphorical meaning: first, metaphorical meaning for \"prolonging days\". Now what about \"Children\"? But you're the children of the Father. Even if you were to take that interpretation, you're not the children of the Son. But I'm even going to accept that. But in the end, do you see the problem you're having now? When you start interpreting the parts you don't like metaphorically, I can do the same thing with the parts you like! I've already given you an explanation: I can already tell you this is referring to the children of Israel. You can interpret anything metaphorically if you like.", "These verses, when you read them completely, I can easily say this is referring to the children of Israel. They were bruised, they suffered, they had all of these issues, they were offered a sheep to their oppressors when they were oppressing them and killing them, like a lamb led to the slaughter. That's a common term, by the way, a lamb led to the slaughter. Christians think it's just about Jesus being said. No! It's a common term used. Even from the time of Abraham it's a common term used: when that ram was given as a sacrifice, this is just a common term, you're bringing a ram to slaughter. But because Christians, they go to John the Baptist, when he said this is the ram of God, and they bring all of this. You read in the lenses of the New Testament; that's why these things become: \"OK! That makes a lot of sense.\" But then you come to these problematic parts and you ignore them, like the idea that he does not open his mouth, the idea that he didn't have children. You have to reinterpret, you have to change. In reality none of this is going to convince someone who's rational, who's not biased, that this is Jesus!", "It would come to mind for Christians, is the point I'm trying to make. You Christians, you already have a belief, so it will come in your mind. But if you objectively analyse verse by verse, or word by word, what it says exactly, and you try to fit it to Jesus, it's not going to work.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Shocks Christian Using Old Testament Prophecy! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wm91qI8yjQQ", ((1643, "27:23–27:56"), (1680, "28:00–28:25"), (1728, "28:48–29:37"), (1782, "29:42–30:22"), (1855, "30:55–31:46"), (1929, "32:09–32:59"), (2006, "33:26–33:46"),), id: 4777)
