=== 79.4 Psalm 22 and Zechariah 12: the pierced one
// NODE P09.C04.S04
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR017:1-120 | Farid Responds | 9
#passage(("When Avery first spoke about this, he said that this is what convinced him. Avery uses Zechariah 12, Isaiah 53, whatever. The main one is Psalms 22. I will admit that out of all the verses that Avery uses, this is the closest thing that he presents to what could possibly be used as a Messianic prophecy, unlike the rest of the fluff that he usually uses.", "See this little footnote above the word pierced? When you click on it, it shows you that the earliest manuscripts include like a lion instead. I feel the need to come clean as to exactly what happened here. I saw the first line of the footnote, and I misread it, thinking that this was referring to like a lion, when they were actually referring to the chosen text above. Did I make a mistake? 100%. Yes, I did. When we make mistakes, we have to come clean about us doing so. It's the only way to get the conversation moving forward, and at the end, what we want is guidance for others. However, I can actually make a very strong case for the like a lion reading.", "What does the verse say? Like a lion, or they pierced? And how does that even happen in the first place? We have these two variants. How could such a mistake occur, because these two statements are very different? When you actually return to the Hebrew, it actually becomes quite clear. The word used for like a lion is kari, while pierced is allegedly written as karu. So, mistakes like this, they're completely reasonable if you think about it. Now, those that claim that this is actually saying they pierced, they will simply point out the structure of the sentence, because they pierced actually fits in grammatically. However, there is a major issue with this, which is that if we simply look at both words, kari and karu, we notice that the spelling for like a lion is actually correct, while the other word does not mean they pierced at all, because it has an additional aleph. The way this word is supposed to be spelled is without the aleph. Now, to get around this, the only thing that Christians can say is that there was some sort of archaic spelling in which the aleph is supposed to be included. Of course, there's no clear-cut evidence of this being the case, but it's just a possibility that is brought forward.", "Let's go through both possibilities in order to determine which was the original reading. Possibility number one: karu was known to be written in this way, but there was a writing convention that adds an [aleph]; then, scribes accidentally shortened the vav at the end, turning it into a yod. The other possibility is that it was originally kari, no special writing convention, and that the yod was unintentionally lengthened into a vav. This possibility makes more sense, because it has less steps.", "Furthermore, if we actually look into the context, the usage of the word lion as a metaphor does actually fit in, despite the awkward grammar. \"Many bulls surround me, strong bulls of Bashan encircle me, roaring lions that tear their prey open their mouths wide against me.\" And then carrying on to 16: \"dogs surround me, a pack of villains encircles me\", and then of course \"my hands and my feet\". And then later on, verse 20: \"deliver me from the sword, my precious life from the power of the dogs, rescue me from the mouth of the lions, save me from the horns of the wild oxen.\" So as we can see, beasts are brought up multiple times. It's not strange for lions to be brought up here, since they were brought up twice in the chapter already.", "Why would the words like a lion even be here? Well, according to Rashi, \"like a lion my hands and feet\" means as though they are crushed in a lion's mouth. And so did Hezekiah say in Isaiah 38:13: \"like a lion so it would break all my bones.\" The imagery is identical to what we find in Isaiah 38, and it becomes clear that the author of Psalms was intentionally alluding to it. Now based on this, we understand why the Masoretic text, the Hebrew Psalms, has like a lion.", "But what about this Dead Sea Scrolls business? Isn't that older? Shouldn't that be the correct and original version? The scribe does include an aleph, of course, but the scribe is not known for using the aleph for vowels. What the scribe is known for, though, is writing the yodh as a vav in other places. Take for example this word: Le'ezrati, which is written as le'ezratu. The last letter in this word is written as a vav, despite the fact that it is supposed to be a yodh. To sum it up, there is a possibility that the Nahal Hever copyist did intend to write like a lion, and not they pierced. Though regardless whether or not that was their intention, the original reading does seem to be like a lion, because of all the reasons that I've previously mentioned.", "But now I do have a final point that I do need to mention, which is that even if we assume that like a lion is not the original reading, they pierced is not a correct translation in the first place. Even if we remove the aleph to give us karu in plural, the singular is kara. This word, according to the Brown-Driver-Briggs Dictionary, means to dig, which is interestingly like the Arabic word for to dig, kara. Ironically, in the KJV the term shows up 16 times. In 12 times, it's translated as to dig. Only in one it is translated as pierced, which is of course what we find in Psalm 22. Despite the Psalms being originally in Hebrew, everyone wants to rely on the Greek translation, Septuagint, since the [extant] manuscripts are earlier. Ironically, the Septuagint does not use the word they pierced either, but uses the word they dug instead.", "The most ironic thing about this whole situation is that it does seem that Avery may have been told that the like a lion variant seems to be the correct one, and the reason why I'm saying this is because he actually defends it. The whole argument is about pierced, and this is showing evidence of the crucifixion. When you switch in pierced with a lion, all the imagery goes away. But then again, Avery uses the most ambiguous, intangible prophecies, like he carried our sorrows, he bore our sins, as explicit and obvious prophecies. So, no wonder he sees Jesus in everything.",), "Farid Responds, “Debunking the Strongest Messianic Prophecy [Response to Godlogic]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HUrVONPwzB0", ((6, "0:06–1:14"), (94, "1:34–3:27"), (211, "3:31–5:04"), (304, "5:04–5:40"), (340, "5:40–6:33"), (401, "6:41–7:19"), (439, "7:19–8:36"), (518, "8:38–10:03"), (606, "10:06–11:19"),), id: 4793)
// U FR059:66-106 | Farid Responds | 1
