ies, I don't expect Christians to love their enemies. I'm saying the biblical Jesus, of course. You cannot claim that Jesus comes and slaughters everyone and he loves them. If this is the Christian version of [love], no wonder that people are being slaughtered throughout history.", "In other parts of the scriptures it says forgive people. I don't have a problem with forgiveness. Islam teaches you to do the same. Allah tells you in the Quran: repel that which is evil with that which is good, meaning if someone does something evil to you, respond to it with something which is good. Then Allah says, then the person that you have enmity with becomes like your closest friend. So that enemy of a person that is acting bad, when you repel his evil with good actions, good behaviors, then he becomes like your closest friend. They say the term \"kill him with kindness\". We don't have a problem with this idea.", "Love is an action of affection and emotion that stems from the heart. Forgiveness is letting go of your right or any feelings of evil. These are two separate things. Love, forgive: two different words, two separate concepts. You cannot love your enemy, because anyways you don't have control over your heart. If someone does all of these things to you, you cannot choose to love them even if you wanted. You can pretend you love them, but you don't truly love them. That's why this teaching makes absolutely no sense.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Convert Attempts To Convince A Muslim Of Christianity! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rsp5eGYtL3Y", ((1123, "18:43–19:16"), (1234, "20:34–21:04"), (1264, "21:04–22:11"), (1331, "22:11–22:54"), (1374, "22:54–23:44"), (1427, "23:47–24:17"), (1488, "24:48–27:10"), (1642, "27:22–27:57"), (1678, "27:58–28:31"),), id: 4820)
