#passage(("What this guy just claimed about the Greek is completely and entirely FALSE. And the reason I didn't argue with him too much about the Greek is because whatever translation he wants to take, it doesn't really give him a strong argument. So I said I'll argue with him either way, for the sake of the argument.", "This verse that we're reading in Corinthians, Paul himself says, 10 verses before (1Cor7:25), that the Creator - God - gave him no commands at all in regards to this issue of virgins and marriage, and that he's just given his opinion. So this entire verse, I could just simply and very easily dismiss it, and say this is just Paul's opinion, and it's not even from God. Why do we need to accept Paul's opinion on this issue?", "But for the sake of the argument anyways, I'm more than happy to talk about the Greek, because the Greek is going to completely destroy his argument. So the Greek word used is ὑπέρακμος (hyperakmos). This is the Greek word used in this specific verse. Now what's interesting is that the majority of the Bible translators translate this verse referring to the man, saying that \"if his passions are strong\".", "Now you have someone called Bruce Winter. He has an entire 14 page scholarly article on this issue titled 'Puberty or Passion'. So he has an entire scholarly article just talking about specifically this verse, and what does this word means. So what does he state in this article? First, he states that this verse has long created an unease between biblical commentators. So they had an issue for years about this specific verse and specific wording. So he quotes, for example, commentaries by scholars like Robertson and Plummer on the Bible, where they say that \"past the flower of her age\" - this translation is very strong for the word (hyperakmos). So it's not fitting.", "He also quotes - which is very important and interesting - this first-century Ephesian doctor that says (hyperakmos) actually means that when the woman is no longer menstruating and can no longer bear children. So \"past the flower of her youth\" means she's no longer youthful. So if you want to take this interpretation, that this is referring to the woman, and you want to take that Greek rendering and understanding in reference to the woman, that would mean that women according to Paul can only marry after the age of 40 or 50, because she's past the flower of her youth. She's no longer menstruating. She is no longer able to bear children.", "He also quotes JS Kistemaker saying that (hyperakmos) actually can refer to both men and women. So that verb is actually gender neutral. It can refer to both men and women. And that it refers to the passion of the man. And then he cites BAGD, which is a well-known Greek English lexicon, as proof. Then Bruce Winter goes to tell us that translators are unsure whether this is referring to the man or the woman. Then he quotes JC Hurd, and JC Hurd was unable to determine if this refers to the passion of the man or the flower of the woman. And then he says this is what JC Hurd says: that the immediate context actually favours that the word refers to the MAN, not the woman. And by the way, of course, in the end, Bruce Winter himself, who wrote this entire article, he concludes in the end that this is in reference to the man, and it has nothing to do with the woman or the age of the woman.", "So to summarise: the Greek word is gender neutral; majority of scholars, and when we talk about the context, they will say that it favors the man, not the woman.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Questions Muslim On Young Marriages Then Instantly Regrets It! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zHZPZujQbd8", ((1225, "20:25–20:38"), (1244, "20:44–21:04"), (1266, "21:06–21:23"), (1290, "21:30–22:04"), (1328, "22:08–22:44"), (1366, "22:46–23:34"), (1420, "23:40–23:46"),), id: 4845)
