#passage(("Bruce Winter has an article called Puberty or Passion, and he deals with this topic quite thoroughly: in their early 20th century commentary, Robertson and [Plummer] suggested that \"past the flower of her age\" is too strong for [hyperakmos]; according to a late 1 Century AD Ephesian doctor, [Soranus], this is when menstruation and thus childbearing ceased; it occurred after the age of 40 and not later than 50.", "Bruce Winter continues: \"Translators themselves are unsure. The Revised English Bible reflects the uncertainty of the gender [referent], giving 'his passions are strong', and for an alternative rendering 'she has reached puberty'.\" There seems to be evidence in Greek literature that either meaning is possible. If the word is genuinely ambiguous, then the immediate context favors referring it to the man, since he is the subject of the preceding verb. Witherington proposes that the young man is full of strong passions for his wife to be, and that the reference of [hyperakmos] is more likely to refer to the man.", "Anyhow, this was the verse that Avery went to in order to make his case. It's his go-to verse, and apparently it doesn't have much going for him.",), "Farid Responds, “Godlogic and Age of Marriage in Bible”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IW5TGYt5fEY", ((260, "4:20–5:00"), (345, "5:45–6:40"), (411, "6:51–6:58"),), id: 4846)
